#
# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the clang-tsan test preset.
#
# The system's OpenSSL is not built with ThreadSanitizer, so its own locking
# around its internal caches (algorithms fetched, keys imported) is invisible,
# and ThreadSanitizer sees only the memcpy and memcmp it intercepts inside
# libcrypto. When a process's first signature checks run on several threads at
# once (evidence verified on all cores), it reports those as races between two
# threads that are both inside libcrypto, on memory libcrypto allocated.
# Accesses made from libcrypto are ignored; races in CexChain's own code are
# still reported.
called_from_lib:libcrypto

# SQLite in WAL mode shares the WAL index (the -shm file, mapped) between the
# writer and its readers without a lock by design: a reader copies the index
# header twice and checks both copies and their checksum, with memory barriers
# between, and retries on a mismatch. ThreadSanitizer sees those copies as
# races with the writer's update of the header and hash tables. Only frames in
# SQLite's WAL code are matched, so a race in our own use of a connection or of
# the reader pool is still reported.
race:sqlite3Wal
